idea:
Replace cycling with a clean-prose back-fill placed at the start of the order. Dedupe, classifier, self-training, heuristics and the damage filter stay unchanged.

(1) Core set: all undamaged docs with final > 0.5, taken in score order up to T*1.01 tokens, as now. If the core alone reaches T, return it shuffled exactly as now (no change).

(2) If the core holds fewer than T tokens, do not cycle and do not use the 0.6*T rule. Fill the remaining budget, up to T*1.01 in total, with the highest-final remaining docs that pass a generic clean-English test:
- not flagged as damaged;
- _common_frac of the first 3000 chars >= 0.8 * the 5th percentile of the target values;
- _alpha_frac of the first 1000 chars >= af_thr;
- zlib ratio of the full doc inside [zr_lo - 0.05, zr_hi + 0.08], which rejects templated coupon spam and random-character junk;
- _cov_min against the raw bigram count table `cnt` >= 0.6 * cov_thr, which rejects word-shuffled text. Keep `cnt` alive until this step.
Run these checks lazily in descending final order, also for docs with score <= 0.3 that the damage filter skipped. Stop when the budget is filled or at t_start + 160 s. Count tokens with tiktoken while time allows, then with the char-ratio estimate.

(3) Order: back-fill docs first (seeded shuffle among themselves), then core docs (seeded shuffle among themselves). The last part of training is then purely in-domain and the off-domain text only serves as early general-English warm-up.

(4) Safety: if no back-fill doc passes, return the shuffled core and let it cycle as now.

Re-use the explicit T-token order with gated back-fill from experiment 7 (lowest dev so far, 1.8993), but make it cheaper and safer in time, and spread the back-fill differently. I do not know why experiments 6 and 7 were rejected despite lower dev bpb than the best; this proposal assumes the cause was the time limit or run-to-run noise, so it is a variant, not a repeat. Dedupe, classifier, self-training, heuristics and the damage filter stay unchanged.

(1) Core = undamaged docs with final > 0.5, in score order, capped at T*1.01 tokens, with per-doc token counts. If the core alone holds >= T tokens, return it shuffled exactly as now.

(2) Back-fill = remaining docs in descending final order, added until core + back-fill reaches 0.6*T tokens. Each candidate must pass the clean-text check of experiment 7:
- not flagged as damaged;
- full-doc zlib ratio inside [zr_lo - 0.03, zr_hi + 0.06];
- _cov_min against the raw count table `cnt` >= 0.6 * cov_thr;
- _common_frac of the first 3000 chars >= 0.8 * the 5th percentile of the target values;
- _alpha_frac of the first 1000 chars >= af_thr.
Keep `cnt`, `cov_thr`, `zr_lo` and `zr_hi` alive for this. The check also runs for docs with classifier score <= 0.3.

(3) Time guards, tighter than experiment 7:
- end the damage filter at t_start + 130 s;
- stop exact tiktoken counting at t_start + 140 s and use the char-ratio estimate after that;
- stop the back-fill gate at t_start + 150 s. After that, accept only docs that pass the cheap common_frac and alpha_frac tests;
- select() must return before t_start + 160 s.

(4) Order (the new part). Let K = the number of core passes needed to reach T*1.01 tokens in total: K = ceil((T*1.01 - backfill_tokens) / core_tokens), with the last pass cut short. Split the back-fill docs at random (seeded) into K-1 equal groups, or one group if K is 1. Pass k, for k = 1..K-1, is the full core plus back-fill group k, seeded shuffle together. The last pass K is core only, freshly shuffled. Return the concatenation. Each back-fill doc is still seen exactly once and the stream never cycles. The off-domain text is spread evenly over all passes except the last, instead of being concentrated in pass 1, and the final stretch is pure in-domain.

(5) Safety: if the core holds fewer than 0.05*T tokens, or no back-fill doc is accepted, return the current shuffled selection.

Use the explicit T-token order with gated back-fill from experiment 7 (lowest dev so far, 1.8993), and halve the amount of back-fill. I do not know why experiments 6, 7 and 9 were rejected despite lower dev bpb than the best; this is a variant that tests the back-fill share, not a repeat. Dedupe, classifier, self-training, heuristics and the damage filter stay unchanged.

(1) Core = undamaged docs with final > 0.5, in score order, capped at T*1.01 tokens, with per-doc token counts. If the core alone holds >= T tokens, return it shuffled exactly as now.

(2) Back-fill budget = 0.5 * max(0, 0.6*T - core_tokens). Experiment 7 used the full gap up to 0.6*T. Fill it with the remaining docs in descending final order. Each candidate must pass the clean-text check of experiment 7, which also runs for docs with classifier score <= 0.3:
- not flagged as damaged;
- full-doc zlib ratio inside [zr_lo - 0.03, zr_hi + 0.06];
- _cov_min against the raw count table `cnt` >= 0.6 * cov_thr;
- _common_frac of the first 3000 chars >= 0.8 * the 5th percentile of the target values;
- _alpha_frac of the first 1000 chars >= af_thr.
Keep `cnt`, `cov_thr`, `zr_lo` and `zr_hi` alive for this. If the damage-filter statistics are unavailable, use only the common_frac and alpha_frac tests.

(3) Order:
- Pass 1: core + accepted back-fill, seeded shuffle together.
- Pass 2, 3, ...: core only, with a fresh seeded shuffle each pass, appended until the running total reaches T*1.01 tokens. The last pass may be cut short.
The trainer never cycles. Each back-fill doc is seen once and the rest of the budget is in-domain.

(4) Time guards, as in experiment 9:
- end the damage filter at t_start + 130 s;
- stop exact tiktoken counting at t_start + 140 s and use the char-ratio estimate after that;
- stop the back-fill gate at t_start + 150 s, then accept only docs that pass the cheap common_frac and alpha_frac tests;
- return before t_start + 160 s.

(5) Safety: if the core holds fewer than 0.05*T tokens, or no back-fill doc is accepted, return the current shuffled selection.

Add the back-fill quality gate from experiment 7 to the current best, but do NOT build an explicit repeated order: return each index at most once and let the trainer cycle the list as it does now. I do not know why experiments 6, 7, 9 and 10 were rejected despite lower dev bpb; the feature they all share is an explicit T-token order with repeated indices and extra late work, so this proposal isolates the gate without either. Dedupe, classifier, self-training, heuristics, core damage filter and final single shuffle stay unchanged.

(1) Core = undamaged docs with final > 0.5, in score order, capped at T*1.01 tokens, as now. If the core reaches T tokens, return it shuffled as now.

(2) Back-fill = the docs the existing min_tokens = 0.6*T rule adds after the core (final <= 0.5). Walk them in descending final order. Each candidate must pass a lazy clean-text check, which also runs for docs with classifier score <= 0.3 that the damage filter never examined. Keep `cnt`, `cov_thr`, `zr_lo` and `zr_hi` alive for it. A candidate passes if:
- it is not flagged as damaged;
- its full-doc zlib ratio is inside [zr_lo - 0.03, zr_hi + 0.06];
- its _cov_min against the raw bigram count table `cnt` is >= 0.6 * cov_thr;
- _common_frac of its first 3000 chars is >= 0.8 * the 5th percentile of the target values;
- _alpha_frac of its first 1000 chars is >= af_thr.
Skip docs that fail and continue down the ranking until core + accepted back-fill reaches 0.6*T tokens. If the damage-filter statistics are unavailable, use only the common_frac and alpha_frac tests.

(3) Output: core + accepted back-fill, one seeded shuffle, no repeated indices. The trainer cycles this list of about 0.6*T tokens exactly as it does for the current best.

(4) Time guards, tighter than now so the run stays well inside 180 s on a slower machine:
- classifier vectorisation budget 60 s instead of 70 s;
- end the damage filter at t_start + 120 s;
- stop exact tiktoken counting at t_start + 135 s and use the char-ratio estimate after that;
- stop the back-fill gate at t_start + 145 s, then accept only docs that pass the cheap common_frac and alpha_frac tests;
- return before t_start + 150 s.

(5) Safety: if no back-fill doc passes, return the shuffled core and let it cycle.

Use the explicit T-token order with gated back-fill from experiment 7 (lowest dev so far, 1.8993) and test the back-fill share in the other direction: raise the unique-token target from 0.6*T to 0.75*T. Experiment 10 halved the back-fill and scored slightly higher (1.9012), so this is a variant, not a repeat. Dedupe, classifier, self-training, heuristics and the core damage filter stay unchanged.

(1) Core = undamaged docs with final > 0.5, in score order, capped at T*1.01 tokens, with per-doc token counts from the existing tiktoken / char-ratio counting. If the core alone holds >= T tokens, return it shuffled exactly as now.

(2) Back-fill = remaining docs in descending final order, added until core + back-fill reaches 0.75*T tokens (experiment 7 used 0.6*T). Each candidate must pass the lazy clean-text check of experiment 7, which also runs for docs with classifier score <= 0.3. Keep `cnt`, `cov_thr`, `zr_lo` and `zr_hi` alive for it. A candidate passes if:
- it is not flagged as damaged;
- its full-doc zlib ratio is inside [zr_lo - 0.03, zr_hi + 0.06];
- its _cov_min against the raw bigram count table `cnt` is >= 0.6 * cov_thr;
- _common_frac of its first 3000 chars is >= 0.8 * the 5th percentile of the target values;
- _alpha_frac of its first 1000 chars is >= af_thr.
Skip docs that fail and continue down the ranking. If the damage-filter statistics are unavailable, use only the common_frac and alpha_frac tests.

(3) Order, as in experiment 7:
- Pass 1: core + accepted back-fill, one seeded shuffle together.
- Pass 2, 3, ...: core only, with a fresh seeded shuffle each pass, appended until the running total reaches T*1.01 tokens. The last pass may be cut short.
The trainer never cycles the mixed list. Each back-fill doc is seen once and the rest of the budget is in-domain repeats.

(4) Time guards, as in experiment 9:
- end the damage filter at t_start + 130 s;
- stop exact tiktoken counting at t_start + 140 s and use the char-ratio estimate after that;
- stop the back-fill gate at t_start + 150 s, then accept only docs that pass the cheap common_frac and alpha_frac tests;
- return before t_start + 160 s.

(5) Safety: if the core holds fewer than 0.05*T tokens, or no back-fill doc is accepted, return the current shuffled selection and let it cycle.
